================================================================================
INVARIANT - anything CX-backed
================================================================================
The CX layer resolves a ContextId to a module's user_data pointer ONCE and
reuses that same pointer. So a module must never move a surviving item's
storage once a ContextId has been handed out for it - no compaction on
remove; free slots become tombstones, reused only by a NEW item with a new
identity.

================================================================================
ENTITY PATTERN - params/categories are the reference implementation
================================================================================
Any new CX-backed object should follow this rather than re-deriving it:
1. Flat, tombstoned storage. Nesting is NOT nesting the storage.
2. uid minted by the owning module from a file-static, never-reset counter,
   so it is unique program-wide. 0 = "none" / "mint me one".
3. Opaque handle = pointer to the entity's own struct carrying self_container
   + self_local_id, plus a resolve function. See param_get_handle /
   param_handle_resolve and mirror it; do not invent a second convention.
4. Parent/child is a parent_uid FIELD on the entity - that is what makes a
   move a field write instead of a storage move.
5. app_data wraps each entity KIND in one DataOps table taking only the
   handle, so every owner shares it (cx_param_ops, cx_param_cat_ops).
6. ContextId = MAKE_ID(one namespace per KIND, uid). Per kind, never per
   owner - rule 2 makes uids program-unique.
7. Structural change = a generation counter on the store; app_data polls it
   and emits DATA_EVENT_CHILDREN_CHANGED; app_intrf diffs the whole subtree.

================================================================================
PLAN
================================================================================
1. PLUGIN PRESET BROWSING, with categories.
   Presets are a list (list_count/list_at) on the loaded plugin, never CX
   nodes. Category rows drill in. The old preset functions in plugins.c /
   clap_plugins.c have no callers - replace them, no twins.
   NAMING TRAP: preset categories (a path from the preset factory / LV2
   bank) have nothing to do with parameter categories (PRM_PARAM_CATEGORY).
   Decided: explicit branch argument; discovery order, no sort pass; one
   CLAP index per plugin instance.

1a. DONE. intern_table: hash index for intern_find (no API change). Presets can
    be 10k+ names, a linear scan makes a rebuild O(n^2).

1b. DONE. Contract: DATA_CHOICE_BRANCH row flag (a category - picking it opens
    it, never reaches action_do) + explicit uint64_t branch argument on
    list_count/list_at (0 = top), passed through data_object.h, app_intrf
    and ui_layer. Existing lists ignore it.

1c. DONE. util_funcs/tree_index, shared by LV2 and CLAP. Opaque:
    tree_index_new/free, every function takes NULL as empty. The owner walks
    its own path syntax: tree_index_branch(parent, name) per level, then
    tree_index_leaf(branch, name, identity), then tree_index_finish. Per
    level sub-categories first, then presets, both in discovery order,
    O(1) level_at, empty categories left out. Keys minted by two intern
    tables that survive tree_index_reset. tree_index_leaf_identity gives
    the identity back - it is all a module keeps per preset (no parallel
    arrays; add per preset data only if a real need appears).

1d. DONE. LV2: build the index once (lilv_plugin_get_related once,
    rdfs:label from the manifest, load_resource only when the label is
    missing, pset:bank label as the category). level_count/level_at/load by
    key/invalidate on the void *plug handle. Symbol -> port table for
    restore. Known limit: a pset:bank only inside a preset's own file is
    seen only when that file had to be read for the label.

1e. CLAP: rewrite clap_ext_preset_factory into one crawl pass (one readdir
    per dir, d_type) adding each preset straight to the tree index.
    Identity = location + "\x1f" + load_key: the load splits at the FIRST
    \x1f (a load_key can hold anything), an empty location means
    LOCATION_PLUGIN. Fix: several presets per container file, NULL location of
    LOCATION_PLUGIN, uninitialised plugin_id, empty extension = every file,
    skipped first location of the next provider, try the _COMPAT factory
    id. clap_plugins.c: index built lazily per instance and cached, same
    API as LV2, load through the preset-load extension.

1f. DONE (LV2). app_data.c: DATA_LIST_NS_BRANCH (category rows of any
    list) + DATA_LIST_NS_LV2_PRESET, static LIST_LV2_PRESETS, SET_CHOICE
    "Preset" on lv2_plugin_ops. Always enabled - action_list runs every
    frame and must never index. Helpers list_branch_key /
    list_choice_from_tree_row are shared with CLAP. No per plugin rescan
    action for LV2: new preset bundles only enter the lilv world through
    the catalogue Scan (plug_plugin_list_init), which now also makes every
    loaded plugin read its presets again. The CLAP half (namespace, list,
    action) lands with 1e.
    Also DONE: LV2 property params follow the plugin - patch:Set and
    patch:Put (the answer to patch:Get) on its output update them (property
    URID -> val_id table, bsearch on rt). param_rt_resend (params.c) sends
    the values a full rt_to_ui queue refused; LV2 plugins and the JACK
    transport call it per cycle, CLAP recovers with
    clap_plug_params_sync_values instead. Control port params of plugins
    with opaque state (JUCE, Pianoteq) cannot follow - LV2 gives no way.

1g. DONE. CLI: LIST_SESSION keeps a stack per spec of LIST_LEVEL {branch,
    parent LIST_CURSOR, label copy}, LIST_DEPTH_MAX 16. l on a category
    pushes, h/ESC pops and restores the cursor (cancel at the top).
    Category rows end in "/", the title shows the path ("Preset / Bank").
    A new CONNECT source empties the target list's stack.

    LV2 first (1d, 1f, 1g) proves the contract end to end before 1e.

    MEMO, after CLAP presets work: param_find_owner_id is a linear scan and
    runs on the [audio-thread] for every CLAP output param event
    (clap_plugins.c, output events loop). Check whether real plugins send a
    burst of output events on preset load. If so: an owner_id -> val_id
    index built on [main-thread], read-only on rt, never rebuilt while rt
    reads it (hash_table.c or a sorted array - not intern_table, the keys
    are integers).

2. CLAP + LV2 optimisation / cleanup.
    CLAP - needs critical extensions implemented, that will touch other parts of the code too.
    Latency, posix_fd_support, timer_support, optional wakefd. Others that plugins like Surge requests.

2a. CLAP GUI GROUNDWORK (Linux). Plugins borrow the host's [main-thread] loop.
   The CLI scanf/fgets prompts block it and would freeze plugin GUIs.

2b. posix-fd-support: register/modify/unregister_fd fill a per plugin fd
    table; clap_read_rt_to_ui_messages polls it (timeout 0) and calls on_fd.
    on_fd may unregister - mark dead, sweep after. Drop fds on destroy.

2c. timer-support: register/unregister_timer fill a per plugin {id, period,
    next_due} table (CLOCK_MONOTONIC); due timers get on_timer in
    clap_read_rt_to_ui_messages. Resolution = the UI's tick.

2d. Optional wake fd: ui_layer_wake_fd() (-1 = none) - an epoll fd holding
    the plugin fds, a timerfd per timer and a periodic rt->ui timerfd.
    Readable -> call ui_layer_update_cycle, never read it. A UI may ignore it
    and just tick.

3a. PRESET SAVING for LV2 and CLAP plugins - save one plugin's current
    state as a preset of that plugin, loadable through the preset list.
    Not step 3: a preset is one plugin's sound, reused across songs; step 3
    is the song file.
    LV2: lilv_state_new_from_instance + lilv_state_save into a ~/.lv2
    bundle, then load that bundle into the world and re-read the plugin's
    preset list so it shows at once. Also the only way to get presets for
    plugins that expose none over LV2 (Pianoteq keeps its own .fxp files).
    CLAP: no standard "save into the plugin's preset folder" - the host
    saves the state extension's blob to its own preset file and lists it
    alongside the plugin's presets (e.g. a user category), loads it back
    through the state extension.

3. SAVING (the song file). Deliberately after (1) and (2): do not design
   the save format until what it must save has stopped moving - presets
   change what a plugin's restorable state even is, and the cleanup may
   change the param/state surface.
   NEVER serialise a params.c uid or a ContextId: the uid counter restarts
   at 0 every launch, so uid 7 is a different param next run. Save DOMAIN
   identity - plugin URI, the plugin's own param id (owner_id), sample path,
   port name. Every owner now supplies a real owner_id; LV2's is the control
   index and must be mapped to plug->controls[owner_id - 1]->symbol before
   writing, never persisted as the index.

4. CX_MOVED + reparenting. DEFERRED - no trigger yet.
   Only buys a non-destructive rename/move IN PLACE, which needs user-
   authored categories. 
   If built: detection is nearly free from app_intrf_cx_resync's existing
   diff ("in both, different parent"); CxEvent needs no new field (a move
   carries the OLD parent/index, like CX_REMOVED); the hashtable entry does
   not change (keyed by data_id, which is what did not change); the apply
   order INVERTS to ADD -> MOVE -> REMOVE (additions first so a move's
   destination exists, removals last so survivors are moved out of doomed
   subtrees) - getting that backwards silently frees live nodes. ui_layer
   cost is ~3 lines: mark the view touched, no pruning.

5. SLOT-TABLE EXTRACTION. DEFERRED - no trigger yet.
   The tribal rules above (1-3) are not enforced anywhere; getting one wrong
   is a silent use-after-free. Extract into util_funcs/ when a THIRD growable
   identity-bearing entity appears (a pattern/clip list, a modulation matrix,
   user macros). Not before: the only two growable ones are params and
   categories, both in params.c, and they already disagree on whether removal
   exists - so which behaviour is "general" is not yet knowable.

